May

May is meant to be a smart contract language where the compiler understands
the model, the states and the transitions before it emits code.

A lot of contract bugs are really state bugs. The code moves from one
situation to another, but the transition is implicit, scattered around the
program or only explained in somebody's head.

May is trying the opposite approach. Start with the model. Give the states
names. Put the transition in the fn signature. Then put the important
rules in the program itself, where the compiler can see them.

Not in a comment above the fn. Not in some separate document that gets
stale the moment the code changes.

So a May program has models, named states, fns and `must` blocks.
The `must` blocks are the interesting part: they are the facts the compiler
should parse, bind to actual names and types, prove statically when it can,
then lower into runtime checks when the target needs them.

The end goal is not just nicer syntax for a chain backend. The goal is a
compiler pipeline that refuses to produce deployable artifacts when the
declared model, the declared transition and the emitted program disagree.

Algorand is the first backend. Other targets should be able to use the same
checked contract model without changing what the source program means.

## What May should prove

The verifier should treat a contract as declarations plus transitions, not
as a loose bag of callable entrypoints.

each declaration owns a block of facts:

    model Counter
        |
        v
    value >= 0

    state Ready(Counter)
        |
        v
    value >= 0

    fn increment(...)
        |
        v
    amount > 0
    after.value == before.value + amount

A `must` block is useful only if the whole stack can be true at the same
time:

    fact_0
    fact_1
    ...
    fact_n
    ------  Z3
    ok

So this is bad:

    must [ x > 10, x < 5 ]

because the verifier reduces it to:

    x > 10
    x < 5
    ------  Z3
    no

The end goal is transition preservation. May should take the input state,
the fn bounds and the body effects as premises:

    before.value >= 0
    amount > 0
    after.value = before.value + amount
    -----------------------------------  every call
    after.value >= 0

The line means: if the top is true, the bottom must be true too.

An SMT solver checks that by asking whether the opposite is possible:

    before.value >= 0
    amount > 0
    after.value = before.value + amount
    after.value < 0
    -----------------------------------  find values?
    no

If the opposite is impossible, the transition preserves the target state for
every symbolic input. If it is possible, the program made a promise the body
does not keep.

The intended pipeline is boring on purpose:

    source
      |
      v
    parse
      |
      v
    bind names and types
      |
      v
    prove must blocks
      |
      v
    prove transitions
      |
      v
    emit target artifacts

code emission belongs at the bottom. A backend should not get to hide a bad
model.

## Running

The command notes are in RUNNING.md.

## Status

The impl is still small on purpose. Types are limited. Expressions
are limited. The backend is only for the subset that the verifier can make
sense of.

That is the point: get a boring, understandable pipeline first.
Parse the program, bind the names, verify the facts, then emit something
only when those steps agree.
